12
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:53,720
On November 10, 1975, the 29 crew members

13
00:00:53,721 --> 00:00:57,860
of the 729-foot ore carrier, Edmund Fitzgerald,

14
00:00:57,861 --> 00:01:01,320
were fighting an intense storm on Lake Superior.

15
00:01:01,321 --> 00:01:04,600
Their destination that day was the protected area of Whitefish

16
00:01:04,601 --> 00:01:08,160
Bay on Michigan's Upper Peninsula.

17
00:01:08,161 --> 00:01:10,740
We're at Whitefish Point just in front of the light station.

18
00:01:10,741 --> 00:01:13,000
Today, the winds are only about 20 miles an hour.

19
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:15,840
Can you imagine what it would have been like with 96-mile-an-hour

20
00:01:15,841 --> 00:01:16,541
winds?

21
00:01:16,542 --> 00:01:18,620
These waves are only about four or five feet today,

22
00:01:18,621 --> 00:01:21,480
but they were 35 feet back in 1975.

23
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:26,220
When the storm had passed, the Edmund Fitzgerald

24
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:28,720
was to deliver its iron ore to its final destination

25
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:31,220
in Detroit's steel mills, where the ore would be used

26
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:32,220
to make cars.

27
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:37,760
Despite the storm, the men were confident in their safety

28
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:39,960
because the Fitzgerald was one of the strongest and most

29
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:42,880
capable ships on the lakes.

30
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:46,180
This was a big, powerful boat.

31
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:49,580
And people really wanted to be on the Edmund Fitzgerald.

32
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:53,720
This was the one you aspired to serve on.

33
00:01:53,721 --> 00:01:58,420
In fact, when it was launched in 1958, it was the premier

34
00:01:58,421 --> 00:02:01,960
ore carrier on the Great Lakes, setting records for the largest

35
00:02:01,961 --> 00:02:04,360
loads carried and the fastest trips.

36
00:02:04,361 --> 00:02:10,160
The captain, Ernest McSorley, was also one of the most

37
00:02:10,161 --> 00:02:13,980
experienced in the business, with 44 years of sailing on the

38
00:02:13,981 --> 00:02:15,980
Great Lakes.

39
00:02:15,981 --> 00:02:20,480
But on November 10, 1975, McSorley was virtually blinded by

40
00:02:20,481 --> 00:02:23,380
was virtually blinded by two critical navigation system

41
00:02:23,381 --> 00:02:25,680
failures.

42
00:02:25,681 --> 00:02:28,720
He reported to a nearby ship that both of his radar units

43
00:02:28,721 --> 00:02:32,440
weren't working, and the radio direction finder was useless

44
00:02:32,441 --> 00:02:36,240
because of an outage at a station on shore.

45
00:02:36,241 --> 00:02:39,040
That nearby ship, a few miles behind,

46
00:02:39,041 --> 00:02:42,440
was the Arthur Andersen, a slightly larger carrier also

47
00:02:42,441 --> 00:02:45,420
trying to reach Whitefish Bay.

48
00:02:45,421 --> 00:02:48,580
Captain Bernie Cooper, using the Andersen's radar,

49
00:02:48,581 --> 00:02:51,080
was radioing General Compass headings to the Fitzgerald's

50
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:54,480
crew.

51
00:02:54,580 --> 00:02:57,680
The pilot house of a ship nearly identical to the Fitzgerald,

52
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:00,920
the William Clay Ford at the Dawson Great Lakes Museum in

53
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:03,720
Detroit, gives a sense of what the captain and crew of the

54
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:08,240
Fitzgerald were dealing with on November 10.

55
00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:10,540
This is the radar unit on the William Clay Ford.

56
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:13,780
It's very similar to the one that was on the Edmund Fitzgerald.

57
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:16,640
The Edmund Fitzgerald's radar unit, however, that night was

58
00:03:16,641 --> 00:03:17,540
was out.

59
00:03:17,541 --> 00:03:21,840
For whatever reason, both units didn't work.

60
00:03:21,841 --> 00:03:24,620
Why both of the Fitzgerald's radar units didn't work

61
00:03:24,621 --> 00:03:27,360
remains a mystery.

62
00:03:27,361 --> 00:03:28,820
There were no reports of problems

63
00:03:28,821 --> 00:03:31,060
before the ship left port.

64
00:03:31,061 --> 00:03:34,020
It's possible that the huge waves or high winds

65
00:03:34,021 --> 00:03:38,360
disabled the antennas on the Fitzgerald's roof.

66
00:03:38,361 --> 00:03:40,200
But the Anderson battled the same storm

67
00:03:40,201 --> 00:03:43,060
without any radar problems.

68
00:03:43,061 --> 00:03:45,540
The other option they had was to use the radio direction

69
00:03:45,541 --> 00:03:47,780
With this, they could triangulate their course.

70
00:03:47,880 --> 00:03:51,280
However, the Whitefish Point sending station

71
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:53,780
was out of service.

72
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:56,180
On shore, the radio direction beacon

73
00:03:56,280 --> 00:03:58,780
at the Whitefish Point lighthouse didn't work.

74
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:02,260
The storm had knocked out power, and the backup generator

75
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:05,200
didn't kick in as it was supposed to.

76
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:10,200
These failures may have doomed the Edmund Fitzgerald.

77
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:14,500
At about 3.30 in the afternoon, McSorley reported to Cooper

78
00:04:14,501 --> 00:04:17,400
that one of the fence rails, part of the sturdy safety

79
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:21,000
fences on each side of the Fitzgerald, had snapped.

80
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:23,500
He also said two vent covers were missing,

81
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:27,440
and the ship was listing to the side, evidence that water was

82
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:30,440
entering the Fitzgerald.

83
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:34,460
These were all signs of severe damage, but the ship soldiered

84
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:38,900
on, pumping out water as it went.

85
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,860
Then at 7.10 PM, the first mate aboard the Arthur Anderson

86
00:04:42,861 --> 00:04:46,860
asked McSorley how the men of the Fitzgerald were doing.

87
00:04:46,861 --> 00:04:49,860
The response back from Fitzgerald to the Arthur

88
00:04:49,861 --> 00:04:50,860
Anderson's, we are holding our own.

89
00:04:50,861 --> 00:04:53,860
That was the last communication between the two ships.

90
00:04:53,861 --> 00:04:56,860
Minutes later, the Edmund Fitzgerald disappeared

91
00:04:56,861 --> 00:04:59,860
from the radar screen aboard the Arthur Anderson without as

92
00:04:59,861 --> 00:05:03,860
much as a distress signal. After a difficult and limited

93
00:05:03,861 --> 00:05:07,860
search during the storm, and a larger search the next day,

94
00:05:07,861 --> 00:05:11,860
it was clear the unthinkable had happened.

95
00:05:11,861 --> 00:05:15,300
It looks from the information that we have that it's

96
00:05:15,301 --> 00:05:20,600
fairly certain that the Fitzgerald went down.

97
00:05:20,601 --> 00:05:24,300
And we're talking now about a matter of life and death

98
00:05:24,301 --> 00:05:25,800
and looking forward to the women's-

99
00:05:25,801 --> 00:05:29,620
The mass of Edmund Fitzgerald and a crew of 29 men

100
00:05:29,621 --> 00:05:30,420
were gone.

101
00:05:30,421 --> 00:05:33,320
We can only ask the masters to do it.

102
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:39,160
Six months later, the cameras of a remotely operated

103
00:05:39,161 --> 00:05:43,900
U.S. Navy submersible spotted the wreckage, agonizingly close to

104
00:05:43,901 --> 00:05:48,160
safety, just 17 miles from Whitefish Point.

105
00:05:48,161 --> 00:05:51,600
Those first images of the wreck, and footage from many other dives

106
00:05:51,601 --> 00:05:54,900
over the years, showed that the ship lay in two pieces on the

107
00:05:54,901 --> 00:05:59,120
bottom of Lake Superior, with its back, or stern, resting

108
00:05:59,121 --> 00:06:02,820
upside down.

109
00:06:02,821 --> 00:06:06,360
The ore pellets from the cargo hold were strewn about, and the

110
00:06:06,361 --> 00:06:10,600
the ship had disintegrated into small pieces.

111
00:06:10,601 --> 00:06:13,600
But finding the wreck didn't provide answers to the questions

112
00:06:13,601 --> 00:06:17,160
about what caused the Fitzgerald to sink, and why the crew never

113
00:06:17,161 --> 00:06:21,600
called for help. More than three decades later,

114
00:06:21,601 --> 00:06:24,660
strong opinions still surround the theories of the ship's

115
00:06:24,661 --> 00:06:28,660
tragic fate. A Coast Guard investigative board

116
00:06:28,661 --> 00:06:32,720
looked into many possibilities. Probably the only one that they

117
00:06:32,721 --> 00:06:35,660
could confidently rule out once the wreck was discovered was

118
00:06:35,661 --> 00:06:38,960
discovered was the possibility that the Fitzgerald had broken

119
00:06:38,961 --> 00:06:41,700
in half on the surface.

120
00:06:41,701 --> 00:06:44,900
The ship didn't break in half because the bow would have

121
00:06:44,901 --> 00:06:47,100
floated for a while longer, and the stern would have floated

122
00:06:47,101 --> 00:06:49,540
for a while, and they both would have disappeared a little bit

123
00:06:49,541 --> 00:06:51,400
further apart.

124
00:06:51,401 --> 00:06:53,220
Instead, the two pieces of the ship

125
00:06:53,221 --> 00:06:56,420
lay only a few hundred feet apart.

126
00:06:56,421 --> 00:06:59,660
In terms of official findings, the Coast Guard report issued

127
00:06:59,661 --> 00:07:05,020
on July 26, 1977, concluded that water crashing on the deck

128
00:07:05,021 --> 00:07:07,660
through cracked and poorly sealed hatch covers was the most

129
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:09,020
likely cause of the disaster.

130
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:15,360
The seals on freighter hatch covers were often damaged

131
00:07:15,460 --> 00:07:18,360
during loading and unloading, and crews often skimped on the

132
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:23,480
tedious process of tightening most of the hatch cover clamps.

133
00:07:23,580 --> 00:07:27,380
Still, many people disagreed with the Coast Guard's theory.

134
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:30,120
If water was leaking in through the hatch covers, then the

135
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:33,120
pumps that were on, these 7,000 gallon per minute pumps, and

136
00:07:33,121 --> 00:07:35,920
and there was two of them going, should have been able to keep up

137
00:07:35,921 --> 00:07:38,220
with as much water that these hatch covers would allow

138
00:07:38,221 --> 00:07:42,660
through leaking. The Lake Carriers Association,

139
00:07:42,661 --> 00:07:45,980
which represented 15 shipping companies, protested to the

140
00:07:45,981 --> 00:07:49,120
National Transportation Safety Board, which issued its own

141
00:07:49,121 --> 00:07:56,080
findings on May 4, 1978. The NTSB also focused on the cargo

142
00:07:56,081 --> 00:08:02,060
hatches, but in a different way. It felt the tremendous weight

143
00:08:02,061 --> 00:08:05,420
the water crashing across the Fitzgerald's main deck, known as

144
00:08:05,421 --> 00:08:09,000
the spar deck, forced one or more of the hatches to implode

145
00:08:09,001 --> 00:08:12,900
and swiftly flood the cargo hold.

146
00:08:12,901 --> 00:08:15,000
You can tell by the size of these things,

147
00:08:15,001 --> 00:08:19,000
this 729-foot freighter was, these things weigh tons, and it

148
00:08:19,001 --> 00:08:21,520
would take an awful lot of weight to cause them to implode.

149
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:27,660
Finally, the Lake Carriers Association proposed its own

150
00:08:27,661 --> 00:08:31,300
theory, that the Fitzgerald struck bottom in the shallow

151
00:08:31,301 --> 00:08:35,300
waters around Caribou Island, a plausible consequence of the

152
00:08:35,301 --> 00:08:38,900
ship's navigation system failures.

153
00:08:38,901 --> 00:08:41,400
Hitting bottom could have caused a rupture in the hull

154
00:08:41,401 --> 00:08:44,700
below the waterline. The downed fence rail and

155
00:08:44,701 --> 00:08:48,000
missing vents the McSorley reported near Caribou Island

156
00:08:48,001 --> 00:08:51,360
seemed to bolster that scenario.

157
00:08:51,361 --> 00:08:54,960
This was serious. How serious?

158
00:08:54,961 --> 00:08:57,660
Not serious enough that McSorley thought he couldn't

159
00:08:57,661 --> 00:09:00,340
continue on. He had his pumps going.

160
00:09:00,341 --> 00:09:02,080
he thought he had it under control.

161
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:04,320
But as Bernie Cooper pointed out later,

162
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:06,600
it's serious when a fence rail comes down.

163
00:09:06,601 --> 00:09:09,580
That indicates an incredible change in tension

164
00:09:09,581 --> 00:09:11,680
in the hull itself.

165
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:14,980
The type of tension that would result

166
00:09:14,981 --> 00:09:18,120
from the middle of the 729-foot ship hitting bottom.

167
00:09:19,420 --> 00:09:22,640
And if you hog a ship, meaning cause it to bend up in the middle,

168
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:24,840
you can stretch those cables and cause them to snap

169
00:09:24,841 --> 00:09:26,840
because they're supported with stanchions all the way along.

170
00:09:27,140 --> 00:09:28,000
Something's got to give.

171
00:09:28,001 --> 00:09:30,600
But since there are no scrape marks on the portion

172
00:09:30,700 --> 00:09:32,600
of the hull wreckage that can be seen,

173
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:35,600
bottoming out, like the other explanations,

174
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:37,100
remains just a theory.

175
00:09:41,140 --> 00:09:44,040
However, one thing most of these theories agree on

176
00:09:44,140 --> 00:09:46,360
is that the Fitzgerald was taking on water faster

177
00:09:46,460 --> 00:09:48,660
than it could be pumped out.

178
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:52,100
And the more water that came in, the lower the ship would ride

179
00:09:52,200 --> 00:09:54,500
in those high seas.

180
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:56,960
The waves were actually boarding the Fitzgerald

181
00:09:56,961 --> 00:10:00,100
the Fitzgerald and the starboard quarter rolling the full length

182
00:10:00,101 --> 00:10:03,040
of the deck, the spar deck, and hitting the back of the pilot

183
00:10:03,041 --> 00:10:06,180
house and actually forcing this 40-foot high structure

184
00:10:06,181 --> 00:10:06,881
underwater.

185
00:10:06,882 --> 00:10:08,960
And it would come back up and shake the water off the deck

186
00:10:08,961 --> 00:10:10,100
and keep going.

187
00:10:10,101 --> 00:10:13,260
But one of those dives that did under one of these huge waves

188
00:10:13,261 --> 00:10:16,220
was the final dive, and it just kept going 535 feet

189
00:10:16,221 --> 00:10:18,860
to the bottom.

190
00:10:18,861 --> 00:10:21,200
If McSorley believed the ship would resurface

191
00:10:21,201 --> 00:10:24,900
after that final wave, then the first sign of trouble,

192
00:10:24,901 --> 00:10:28,800
And the last would have been a wall of water bursting through

193
00:10:28,801 --> 00:10:31,800
the windows of the pilothouse from the increased pressure of

194
00:10:31,801 --> 00:10:33,600
deeper waters.

195
00:10:33,601 --> 00:10:38,400
This would explain why there had been no distress call.

196
00:10:38,401 --> 00:10:41,200
But there's still no single theory about the tragic fate of

197
00:10:41,201 --> 00:10:45,700
the Edmund Fitzgerald that will satisfy everyone.

198
00:10:45,701 --> 00:10:49,400
The bodies of all 29 crewmen remain entombed in that watery

199
00:10:49,401 --> 00:10:50,600
grave.

200
00:10:50,601 --> 00:10:53,300
And at the request of their families, divers are no

201
00:10:53,301 --> 00:10:55,900
longer permitted near the wreck.

202
00:10:55,901 --> 00:10:58,800
But those relatives do have two special places where they can

203
00:10:58,801 --> 00:11:01,500
feel close to their loved ones.

204
00:11:01,501 --> 00:11:05,240
Just outside the Dawson Great Lakes Museum in Detroit, an

205
00:11:05,241 --> 00:11:07,840
anchor that belonged to the Edmund Fitzgerald prior to its

206
00:11:07,841 --> 00:11:10,340
last voyage is displayed.

207
00:11:10,341 --> 00:11:14,460
It is the centerpiece in the annual November Memorial.

208
00:11:14,461 --> 00:11:20,160
We put 29 lamps out here around it, one for every crew member

209
00:11:20,161 --> 00:11:22,760
on the Edmund Fitzgerald.

210
00:11:22,761 --> 00:11:25,300
And at the Great Lakes Shipwreck Museum at Whitefish

211
00:11:25,301 --> 00:11:29,000
Point, the original bell from the Edmund Fitzgerald is the

212
00:11:29,001 --> 00:11:31,600
centerpiece of the collection.

213
00:11:31,601 --> 00:11:34,800
Known as the voice of the ship, it was recovered from the

214
00:11:34,801 --> 00:11:39,420
wreckage at the request of the families on July 4, 1995.

215
00:11:39,421 --> 00:11:42,220
When this bell broke the surface after 20 years of

216
00:11:42,221 --> 00:11:45,260
darkness and silence, it actually swung and told, and

217
00:11:45,261 --> 00:11:47,620
everybody around for about a mile could hear it.

218
00:11:47,621 --> 00:11:50,960
It was one of the most eerie experiences I've ever had.

219
00:11:50,961 --> 00:11:53,700
and the tone itself has such a resonating quality to it

220
00:11:53,701 --> 00:11:55,560
that is something that you'll never forget.

221
00:12:15,620 --> 00:12:18,220
After the original bell was recovered from the wreckage

222
00:12:18,221 --> 00:12:20,520
of the Edmund Fitzgerald, a replica bell

223
00:12:20,521 --> 00:12:24,020
put in its place. The names of all 29 crew members

224
00:12:24,021 --> 00:12:26,820
were inscribed on the replica, which serves as a permanent

225
00:12:26,821 --> 00:12:29,820
grave marker. Engineering Disasters will

226
00:12:29,821 --> 00:12:39,120
return on Modern Marvels. We now return to Engineering

227
00:12:39,121 --> 00:12:46,020
Disasters on Modern Marvels. About 1 million people

228
00:12:46,021 --> 00:12:51,160
worldwide fly in a Boeing 737 every day.

229
00:12:51,260 --> 00:12:54,120
During its decades of service, the plane has compiled an

230
00:12:54,220 --> 00:12:58,060
impressive safety record. In fact, the 737 quickly

231
00:12:58,160 --> 00:13:01,060
became the workhorse of the commercial airline industry

232
00:13:01,160 --> 00:13:06,480
after its debut in 1968.

233
00:13:06,580 --> 00:13:10,280
But a fatal flaw caused at least two crashes and led to the

234
00:13:10,380 --> 00:13:12,680
longest investigation in the history of the National

235
00:13:12,681 --> 00:13:14,680
Transportation Safety Board.

236
00:13:18,680 --> 00:13:23,920
In 1991, United Flight 585 crashed into a park in Colorado

237
00:13:23,921 --> 00:13:25,720
Springs.

238
00:13:25,721 --> 00:13:28,720
The pilot had been making a routine landing approach at 1,000

239
00:13:28,721 --> 00:13:32,340
feet when the plane suddenly banked to the right, turned

240
00:13:32,341 --> 00:13:38,440
upside down, and plummeted nose first into the ground.

241
00:13:38,441 --> 00:13:42,440
All 25 people on board were killed.

242
00:13:42,540 --> 00:13:46,740
The investigation proved to be especially difficult.

243
00:13:46,840 --> 00:13:48,780
The flight data recorder was an older type

244
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:51,180
that only had five parameters.

245
00:13:51,280 --> 00:13:54,480
So we didn't get a lot of data from this.

246
00:13:54,580 --> 00:13:59,800
The aircraft itself was severely damaged and disintegrated

247
00:13:59,900 --> 00:14:01,400
by the impact.

248
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:04,840
Many of the components were difficult to test.

249
00:14:04,940 --> 00:14:08,240
After nearly two years, the frustrated NTSB

250
00:14:08,241 --> 00:14:11,280
were unable to determine a definitive cause.

251
00:14:11,281 --> 00:14:13,920
Two probable theories surfaced.

252
00:14:13,921 --> 00:14:17,340
It may have been a mountain rotor from winds coming off

253
00:14:17,341 --> 00:14:20,880
the mountain, sort of a horizontal tornado,

254
00:14:20,881 --> 00:14:24,880
or possibly a failure of the aircraft's rudder control

255
00:14:24,881 --> 00:14:25,700
system.

256
00:14:25,701 --> 00:14:29,300
But there wasn't enough information to decide either way.

257
00:14:29,301 --> 00:14:33,840
Then in 1994, an eerily similar second 737

258
00:14:33,841 --> 00:14:35,400
crash occurred.

259
00:14:35,401 --> 00:14:40,760
This time, US Air Flight 427 approached Pittsburgh on a calm

260
00:14:40,761 --> 00:14:44,940
September evening. It passed through some turbulence

261
00:14:44,941 --> 00:14:48,300
from the wake of a plane a few miles ahead, which momentarily

262
00:14:48,301 --> 00:14:52,300
surprised the crew. It should have been easy for

263
00:14:52,301 --> 00:14:56,500
the experienced pilot to make a simple adjustment, but the 737

264
00:14:56,501 --> 00:15:00,900
suddenly banked hard to the left, turned over, and spiraled into

265
00:15:00,901 --> 00:15:01,800
the ground.

266
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:11,000
All 132 people on board died.

267
00:15:11,001 --> 00:15:13,000
Investigators rushed to the scene,

268
00:15:13,001 --> 00:15:15,940
immediately aware of the similarities to the 1991

269
00:15:15,941 --> 00:15:18,440
Colorado Springs crash.

270
00:15:18,441 --> 00:15:21,480
This time, they had more information to work with.

271
00:15:21,481 --> 00:15:24,560
We had more flight data recorder parameters.

272
00:15:24,561 --> 00:15:28,760
And also with US Air 427, the rudder power control unit,

273
00:15:28,761 --> 00:15:30,600
the controls of rudder, was intact.

274
00:15:30,601 --> 00:15:34,960
It was in a testable condition, which we didn't have before.

275
00:15:34,961 --> 00:15:37,460
The rudder of an airplane functions much like the rudder

276
00:15:37,461 --> 00:15:41,100
of a ship, turning the aircraft to its right or left, which is

277
00:15:41,101 --> 00:15:43,360
known as yaw.

278
00:15:43,361 --> 00:15:45,720
Flaps on the wings, known as ailerons,

279
00:15:45,721 --> 00:15:49,900
control the roll of the plane to the left or right.

280
00:15:49,901 --> 00:15:52,840
Adjusting the horizontal stabilizer on the tail

281
00:15:52,841 --> 00:15:57,500
affects the pitch, which raises or lowers the nose of the plane.

282
00:15:57,501 --> 00:16:00,280
But extreme rudder movements can affect more than just the yaw

283
00:16:00,281 --> 00:16:03,160
of an aircraft.

284
00:16:03,161 --> 00:16:06,220
What happens when the rudder reaches its full travel limit

285
00:16:06,221 --> 00:16:10,260
is that the airplane slips through the air sideways.

286
00:16:10,900 --> 00:16:14,560
And as a result, for airplanes that have swept back wings,

287
00:16:15,180 --> 00:16:17,440
one wing develops more lift than in the other,

288
00:16:17,620 --> 00:16:19,260
and the airplane then starts to roll.

289
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:23,940
At relatively low speeds, such as during takeoff,

290
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:27,140
or in the case of both crashes, on approach to landing,

291
00:16:27,760 --> 00:16:30,160
if the extreme rudder position is not corrected,

292
00:16:30,161 --> 00:16:35,120
the rudder will overpower any aileron adjustments and force the aircraft all

293
00:16:35,121 --> 00:16:38,180
the way over, leading to a nosedive into the ground.

294
00:16:40,760 --> 00:16:45,640
But initial tests of the U.S. Air rudder equipment didn't reveal any defects.

295
00:16:46,420 --> 00:16:49,860
Because the U.S. Air crash happened on a clear evening with little wind,

296
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:54,720
adverse weather could be ruled out. The only other possibility seemed to be

297
00:16:54,721 --> 00:16:56,880
pilot error or disorientation.

298
00:16:56,881 --> 00:17:02,480
To test the motion effects recorded from the U.S. air

299
00:17:02,481 --> 00:17:06,920
flight in 1994, investigators went to the NASA Ames Research

300
00:17:06,921 --> 00:17:10,160
Center in Northern California, home of the world's largest

301
00:17:10,161 --> 00:17:15,660
flight simulator. Unlike other simulators that simply

302
00:17:15,661 --> 00:17:19,440
pivot on a stationary platform, the center's vertical motion

303
00:17:19,441 --> 00:17:25,280
simulator can move 60 feet up or down and 40 feet side to side.

304
00:17:25,281 --> 00:17:27,240
The pilots that we've had from all over the world

305
00:17:27,241 --> 00:17:29,820
that have come in to fly the vertical motion simulators

306
00:17:29,821 --> 00:17:32,780
say that it replicates flight like no other simulator

307
00:17:32,781 --> 00:17:33,580
they've ever been in.

308
00:17:36,920 --> 00:17:38,960
Technicians loaded information directly

309
00:17:38,961 --> 00:17:42,180
from US Air 427's flight data recorder

310
00:17:42,181 --> 00:17:44,920
into the simulator's computers.

311
00:17:44,921 --> 00:17:45,840
Excuse me.

312
00:17:45,841 --> 00:17:47,680
Appreciate it.

313
00:17:47,681 --> 00:17:49,680
Operate.

314
00:17:49,681 --> 00:17:52,760
The result, seen here using the same US Air flight

315
00:17:52,761 --> 00:17:56,560
was a chilling reconstruction of what the crew most likely saw

316
00:17:56,660 --> 00:17:57,960
and felt.

317
00:17:58,060 --> 00:18:00,760
As we roll out, there'll be a wake vortex upset

318
00:18:00,860 --> 00:18:05,500
that resulted from a 727 that was flying ahead of the 737.

319
00:18:05,600 --> 00:18:06,800
And that started the maneuver.

320
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:09,340
Right there, you feel the wake vortex upset.

321
00:18:09,440 --> 00:18:12,480
The pilot then tries to right the airplane,

322
00:18:12,580 --> 00:18:16,020
but during that process, the rudder then goes over hard

323
00:18:16,120 --> 00:18:17,020
to the left.

324
00:18:17,120 --> 00:18:20,920
And so as a result, the airplane rolls uncontrollably

325
00:18:20,921 --> 00:18:23,760
uncontrollably to the left and must have been terrifying in

326
00:18:23,761 --> 00:18:24,760
the cockpit.

327
00:18:29,920 --> 00:18:32,300
Nothing from the work in the vertical motion simulator

328
00:18:32,301 --> 00:18:35,560
led investigators and motion experts to believe that pilot

329
00:18:35,561 --> 00:18:40,340
error or disorientation were factors in the crash.

330
00:18:40,341 --> 00:18:43,380
They returned to the rudder control system, but could still

331
00:18:43,381 --> 00:18:46,520
find no sign of malfunction.

332
00:18:46,521 --> 00:18:48,780
Then, a vital clue.

333
00:18:48,781 --> 00:18:52,520
In June 1996, an East Wind Airlines pilot

334
00:18:52,521 --> 00:18:54,680
reported rudder problems.

335
00:18:54,681 --> 00:18:57,780
It seemed like the hydraulic system of his 737

336
00:18:57,781 --> 00:19:00,860
was actually pushing back when he depressed the foot pedal

337
00:19:00,861 --> 00:19:03,660
that controlled the plane's rudder.

338
00:19:03,661 --> 00:19:06,400
Luckily, the plane didn't crash because the incident didn't

339
00:19:06,401 --> 00:19:08,700
occur during takeoff or landing.

340
00:19:08,701 --> 00:19:10,940
This incident led NTSB investigators

341
00:19:10,941 --> 00:19:13,080
to examine how the rudder could have moved

342
00:19:13,081 --> 00:19:15,340
opposite of the pilot's control.

343
00:19:15,341 --> 00:19:17,900
They focused on the hydraulic heart of the system,

344
00:19:17,901 --> 00:19:21,300
the rudder's dual servo valve power control unit.

345
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:23,800
It's about the size of a soda can.

346
00:19:23,900 --> 00:19:27,640
And inside of that is a valve about the side of a pencil

347
00:19:27,740 --> 00:19:30,180
that's got ridges in it, and it moves back and forth

348
00:19:30,280 --> 00:19:32,180
and ports fluid.

349
00:19:32,280 --> 00:19:34,380
That's the primary valve.

350
00:19:34,480 --> 00:19:37,520
Around that is a slightly larger valve, cylindrical

351
00:19:37,620 --> 00:19:38,960
in shape with ports in it.

352
00:19:39,060 --> 00:19:41,920
And that's the secondary valve.

353
00:19:42,020 --> 00:19:43,960
When a rudder is operating normally,

354
00:19:44,060 --> 00:19:47,560
only the inner primary valve is supposed to move.

355
00:19:47,561 --> 00:19:50,400
Depending on which direction a pilot wants to turn,

356
00:19:50,401 --> 00:19:54,060
the hydraulic pressure either extends or retracts a piston,

357
00:19:54,061 --> 00:19:56,660
which then turns the rudder.

358
00:19:56,661 --> 00:19:58,860
Once the pilot's foot is off the pedal,

359
00:19:58,861 --> 00:20:01,060
the pressure is released, and the rudder

360
00:20:01,061 --> 00:20:03,700
returns to a neutral position.

361
00:20:03,701 --> 00:20:05,960
The outer, secondary valve only moves

362
00:20:05,961 --> 00:20:10,560
if the smaller, primary valve becomes stuck.

363
00:20:10,561 --> 00:20:13,500
Virtually all aircraft systems have both the primary and a

364
00:20:13,501 --> 00:20:17,160
backup, an important design feature known as redundancy

365
00:20:17,161 --> 00:20:22,240
in the airline business. But since the 737's rudder power

366
00:20:22,241 --> 00:20:26,160
control unit used two valves within the same hydraulic system,

367
00:20:26,161 --> 00:20:30,340
the valves were not totally independent of each other.

368
00:20:30,341 --> 00:20:35,220
In 1998, investigators discovered how the valves had created the

369
00:20:35,221 --> 00:20:40,340
deadly problem. We saw that if the secondary valve

370
00:20:40,341 --> 00:20:45,820
were to be jammed just slightly off neutral, and you put in a

371
00:20:45,821 --> 00:20:49,620
command into the primary valve, you could misport the fluid,

372
00:20:49,621 --> 00:20:52,820
hydraulic fluid, and cause the rudder to reverse operation.

373
00:20:52,821 --> 00:20:54,820
This would be very similar to driving your car.

374
00:20:54,821 --> 00:20:56,820
You turn to the right, the car goes left.

375
00:20:56,821 --> 00:20:59,820
You probably will keep trying to turn to the right.

376
00:20:59,821 --> 00:21:02,820
You won't figure that out before you go off the road.

377
00:21:02,821 --> 00:21:06,820
The longest NTSB aircraft investigation in history

378
00:21:06,821 --> 00:21:11,820
was finally over, four and a half years after the US Air 737

379
00:21:11,821 --> 00:21:16,320
crash in Pittsburgh. Instructors immediately taught

380
00:21:16,321 --> 00:21:20,360
737 pilots ways to correct the problem, such as momentarily

381
00:21:20,361 --> 00:21:23,360
removing a foot from the pedal to release pressure in the event

382
00:21:23,361 --> 00:21:27,280
of a jam, and switching from the primary to the secondary

383
00:21:27,281 --> 00:21:30,760
hydraulic master control for the entire aircraft in order to

384
00:21:30,761 --> 00:21:35,120
free the rudder. The training continues today

385
00:21:35,121 --> 00:21:37,820
because the new units with two completely separate rudder

386
00:21:37,821 --> 00:21:41,120
systems are still being installed.

387
00:21:41,121 --> 00:21:44,260
In fact, the retrofit process won't be complete until near

388
00:21:44,261 --> 00:21:47,660
the end of the decade. However, the planes are still

389
00:21:47,661 --> 00:21:51,960
safe in the meantime, thanks to extensive pilot training.

390
00:21:51,961 --> 00:21:55,480
And the 737 seems destined to remain the workhorse of the

391
00:21:55,481 --> 00:21:58,880
industry as the design changes are completed.

392
00:22:02,280 --> 00:22:07,380
Since the 737 debuted in 1968, Boeing has built over 5,000

393
00:22:07,381 --> 00:22:08,880
of the planes.

394
00:22:08,881 --> 00:22:12,380
That makes the 737 history's best-selling passenger

395
00:22:12,381 --> 00:22:13,380
airplane.

396
00:22:13,381 --> 00:22:16,380
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

397
00:22:22,380 --> 00:22:26,380
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

398
00:22:29,380 --> 00:22:33,380
In 1959, just 30 miles from downtown Los Angeles,

399
00:22:33,381 --> 00:22:37,380
a little-known nuclear disaster began to unfold at the time

400
00:22:37,381 --> 00:22:42,880
important Cold War facility. The Santa Susana Field Laboratory,

401
00:22:42,881 --> 00:22:45,880
created by the government's Atomic Energy Commission and

402
00:22:45,881 --> 00:22:49,620
operated by Rocketdyne, played a vital defense role by producing

403
00:22:49,621 --> 00:22:56,740
powerful rockets. 2,800-acre site was built in the

404
00:22:56,741 --> 00:23:00,940
hills above Simi Valley, a town of 8,000 just west of Los

405
00:23:00,941 --> 00:23:07,360
Angeles' San Fernando Valley. Besides rockets, the

406
00:23:07,361 --> 00:23:09,940
it was home to several experimental nuclear reactors,

407
00:23:10,480 --> 00:23:13,660
which the Atomic Energy Commission hoped could be commercially viable.

408
00:23:16,680 --> 00:23:19,940
One of them used sodium instead of water as a coolant.

409
00:23:20,760 --> 00:23:24,580
It was known as the Sodium Reactor Experiment, or SRE.

410
00:23:25,440 --> 00:23:29,140
But the experiment would soon trigger a technological nightmare.

411
00:23:30,080 --> 00:23:34,080
This reactor was built in an era of nuclear cowboys.

412
00:23:34,520 --> 00:23:36,980
The Atomic Energy Commission came out of the Manhattan Project.

413
00:23:36,981 --> 00:23:39,140
There was a culture of secrecy.

414
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:41,880
There was a sense of excitement about pushing the limit

415
00:23:41,980 --> 00:23:44,620
on those reactors.

416
00:23:44,720 --> 00:23:48,760
On July 26, 1959, technicians barely

417
00:23:48,860 --> 00:23:51,820
managed to manually shut down the sodium reactor

418
00:23:51,920 --> 00:23:55,400
when temperature and radiation readings quickly jumped.

419
00:23:55,500 --> 00:23:58,600
Then, after two hours of basic inspections,

420
00:23:58,700 --> 00:24:00,440
they made a baffling decision.

421
00:24:00,540 --> 00:24:02,780
They started up the reactor anyway.

422
00:24:02,880 --> 00:24:05,540
They operated the reactor for about two more weeks

423
00:24:05,541 --> 00:24:08,040
before the radiation levels.

424
00:24:08,140 --> 00:24:09,620
And there were so many signs of trouble

425
00:24:09,720 --> 00:24:13,240
that it was thought it was time to stop and get real serious

426
00:24:13,340 --> 00:24:16,820
about finding out what was going wrong.

427
00:24:16,920 --> 00:24:19,960
When they dropped a camera into the reactor core,

428
00:24:20,060 --> 00:24:24,120
they discovered that 13 of 43 radioactive fuel rods were

429
00:24:24,220 --> 00:24:26,960
damaged and partially melted.

430
00:24:27,060 --> 00:24:29,560
And because this was an experimental reactor,

431
00:24:29,660 --> 00:24:31,860
a thick concrete containment structure,

432
00:24:31,960 --> 00:24:34,940
such a familiar and vital part of more modern reactors,

433
00:24:34,941 --> 00:24:38,020
wasn't required. It was just built in an ordinary

434
00:24:38,021 --> 00:24:42,140
industrial building. And in fact, it was designed so that

435
00:24:42,141 --> 00:24:45,420
radioactive gases would be intentionally vented from the

436
00:24:45,421 --> 00:24:50,380
reactor to holding tanks and then out into the environment.

437
00:24:50,381 --> 00:24:53,420
Exactly how much radiation went into the atmosphere is still a

438
00:24:53,421 --> 00:24:56,300
matter of debate, because the monitoring equipment at the

439
00:24:56,301 --> 00:25:01,500
facility could only measure very minor levels of radiation.

440
00:25:01,501 --> 00:25:04,140
This was a much smaller reactor than the one at the site of the

441
00:25:04,141 --> 00:25:09,140
accident in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania in 1979.

442
00:25:09,141 --> 00:25:12,780
But because it didn't have a containment dome, the sodium

443
00:25:12,781 --> 00:25:15,660
reactor might have created a much bigger problem.

444
00:25:15,661 --> 00:25:19,660
The SRE was about one hundredth the size of Three Mile

445
00:25:19,661 --> 00:25:22,620
Island, and the containment structure of Three Mile Island,

446
00:25:22,621 --> 00:25:25,260
according to the official government reports, stayed

447
00:25:25,261 --> 00:25:29,200
intact, and that only a small fraction of the radioactive

448
00:25:29,201 --> 00:25:31,740
inventory were officially estimated to get out of the

449
00:25:31,741 --> 00:25:34,040
out of the Threma Island reactor.

450
00:25:34,041 --> 00:25:38,940
And so several researchers have estimated that the radiation

451
00:25:38,941 --> 00:25:45,480
release from the SRE could be as high as 240 times the radiation

452
00:25:45,481 --> 00:25:48,320
release from Threma Island.

453
00:25:48,321 --> 00:25:50,120
Five weeks after the accident,

454
00:25:50,121 --> 00:25:53,100
the Atomic Energy Commission issued a press release,

455
00:25:53,101 --> 00:25:56,600
claiming that just a single fuel rod had been slightly damaged

456
00:25:56,601 --> 00:25:59,140
and no radiation had been released.

457
00:25:59,141 --> 00:26:02,740
The body of the release said there is no evidence of unsafe

458
00:26:02,741 --> 00:26:06,740
operating conditions. One third of the fuel had

459
00:26:06,741 --> 00:26:09,660
experienced melting. It was one of the worst accidents

460
00:26:09,661 --> 00:26:14,380
in nuclear history, and the AEC essentially lied about it.

461
00:26:14,381 --> 00:26:18,660
In 1961, the Atomic Energy Commission made this film,

462
00:26:18,661 --> 00:26:22,300
which documents the extent of the damage and cleanup process.

463
00:26:24,940 --> 00:26:28,300
It also explained exactly what went wrong inside this unique

464
00:26:28,301 --> 00:26:32,300
where molten, or liquid sodium, was used instead of water

465
00:26:32,301 --> 00:26:35,300
to remove heat from the swimming pool-sized reactor area.

466
00:26:37,300 --> 00:26:40,300
Sodium reactors were being developed because there was a

467
00:26:40,301 --> 00:26:43,300
concern there wasn't enough uranium to fuel all of the

468
00:26:43,301 --> 00:26:46,300
nuclear reactors being built at the time.

469
00:26:46,301 --> 00:26:51,300
Sodium reactors never deplete their original fuel supply.

470
00:26:51,301 --> 00:26:55,300
Water reactors are less efficient since they consume uranium

471
00:26:55,301 --> 00:26:59,300
and eventually need to be resupplied.

472
00:26:59,301 --> 00:27:02,480
But the benefits of liquid sodium came with a price.

473
00:27:02,481 --> 00:27:06,000
DR. SOTOMER

474
00:27:06,001 --> 00:27:08,060
SOTOMERBAUM

475
00:27:08,061 --> 00:27:14,060
Because water couldn't be near the reactor,

476
00:27:14,061 --> 00:27:18,060
a chemical known as tetralin was used to cool the bearings

477
00:27:18,061 --> 00:27:21,060
in the pumps that circulated the sodium as it carried heat

478
00:27:21,061 --> 00:27:24,060
away from the fuel rods.

479
00:27:24,061 --> 00:27:27,360
Everything worked well until tetralyn started leaking

480
00:27:27,460 --> 00:27:31,260
through cracks in the pump seals and into the reactor.

481
00:27:31,360 --> 00:27:35,980
The neutron bombardment inside the reactor core caused it

482
00:27:36,080 --> 00:27:39,440
to turn from an organic chemical into an almost like a glue or a

483
00:27:39,540 --> 00:27:41,980
tar-like substance.

484
00:27:42,080 --> 00:27:44,420
As the tetralyn approached the fuel rods,

485
00:27:44,520 --> 00:27:46,820
it partially blocked the bottom of some of the coolant

486
00:27:46,920 --> 00:27:48,420
channels.

487
00:27:48,520 --> 00:27:51,820
When coolant flow decreased, the heat by those fuel rods

488
00:27:51,821 --> 00:27:55,060
increased and eventually reached melting point.

489
00:27:55,160 --> 00:27:57,560
What happens when the fuel starts melting

490
00:27:57,660 --> 00:28:00,500
is the radioactive byproducts which have been created

491
00:28:00,600 --> 00:28:03,340
from reactor operation have to go somewhere.

492
00:28:03,440 --> 00:28:05,840
And so those radioactive byproducts

493
00:28:05,940 --> 00:28:09,500
go into the molten sodium, and some of those byproducts

494
00:28:09,600 --> 00:28:15,180
come out as a vapor and, in this case, went into the air.

495
00:28:15,280 --> 00:28:17,540
Some of the radioactive materials like strontium

496
00:28:17,640 --> 00:28:21,820
are actually absorbed by the body into the bones and teeth.

497
00:28:21,821 --> 00:28:25,360
so they remain in the body for a long period of time,

498
00:28:25,500 --> 00:28:28,900
all the while releasing radioactivity that can harm cells

499
00:28:28,901 --> 00:28:30,480
in other parts of the body.

500
00:28:31,880 --> 00:28:34,080
Workers who cleaned up after the accident

501
00:28:34,081 --> 00:28:36,620
or worked at the facility once it was restarted

502
00:28:36,621 --> 00:28:38,060
were at highest risk.

503
00:28:39,320 --> 00:28:41,300
The studies that were done of the workers

504
00:28:41,301 --> 00:28:43,620
by a team from the UCLA School of Public Health

505
00:28:43,621 --> 00:28:46,640
found that the workers who had the highest radiation exposures

506
00:28:46,641 --> 00:28:49,560
had tripled the death rates from cancer,

507
00:28:49,561 --> 00:28:53,740
such as cancer of the lung, lymph, and blood systems,

508
00:28:54,340 --> 00:28:58,140
as similar workers at the site who had lower radiation exposures.

509
00:29:00,240 --> 00:29:02,840
The University of California at Los Angeles

510
00:29:02,841 --> 00:29:05,320
was also responsible for bringing the story to the public.

511
00:29:06,900 --> 00:29:09,920
After the Three Mile Island accident in 1979,

512
00:29:10,740 --> 00:29:13,540
some of Daniel Hirsch's students started wondering

513
00:29:13,541 --> 00:29:16,020
if anything like it had ever happened in Southern California.

514
00:29:16,021 --> 00:29:20,860
In UCLA's engineering library, they found documents and

515
00:29:20,861 --> 00:29:24,260
photographs of the melted fuel rods from the sodium reactor

516
00:29:24,261 --> 00:29:27,160
accident at the site run by Rocketdyne's Atomics

517
00:29:27,161 --> 00:29:28,660
International Division.

518
00:29:28,661 --> 00:29:32,260
And by a quirk of fate, it turned out that the man who

519
00:29:32,261 --> 00:29:37,180
had founded and run Atomics International had subsequently

520
00:29:37,181 --> 00:29:41,020
become dean of engineering at UCLA and taken with him box

521
00:29:41,021 --> 00:29:43,180
loads of the old documents.

522
00:29:43,181 --> 00:29:44,680
documents.

523
00:29:44,681 --> 00:29:49,420
We then released that to the press in 1979.

524
00:29:49,421 --> 00:29:56,360
So the AEC kept it secret, and it stayed secret for 20 years.

525
00:29:56,361 --> 00:29:57,920
The Atomic Energy Commission also

526
00:29:57,921 --> 00:30:00,940
managed to conceal accidents at two other reactors at the Santa

527
00:30:00,941 --> 00:30:06,640
Susana facility in the 1960s.

528
00:30:06,641 --> 00:30:09,040
Today, the reactor buildings have been torn down and taken

529
00:30:09,041 --> 00:30:13,040
But years of cleanup work remain.

530
00:30:13,041 --> 00:30:15,040
Health effects on the surrounding communities

531
00:30:15,041 --> 00:30:18,040
have been difficult to study because of their large populations

532
00:30:18,041 --> 00:30:22,040
and the fact that many people have moved out of or into the

533
00:30:22,041 --> 00:30:25,040
area over the years.

534
00:30:25,041 --> 00:30:29,040
And Southern California's population continues to swell.

535
00:30:29,041 --> 00:30:32,040
People are living closer than ever to the site, which is now

536
00:30:32,041 --> 00:30:34,040
owned by Boeing.

537
00:30:34,041 --> 00:30:37,040
And because certain types of radiation released in the

538
00:30:37,041 --> 00:30:40,100
released in the accident can remain at dangerously high

539
00:30:40,101 --> 00:30:43,440
levels for centuries, the decisions made at Santa

540
00:30:43,441 --> 00:30:47,540
Susana in the 1950s and 60s will continue to affect

541
00:30:47,541 --> 00:30:49,780
residents for generations to come.

542
00:30:54,340 --> 00:30:56,700
Of the three most prevalent radioactive elements

543
00:30:56,701 --> 00:31:01,780
identified at the Santa Susana site, cesium-137 has the longest

544
00:31:01,781 --> 00:31:03,740
half-life at 30 years.

545
00:31:03,741 --> 00:31:05,940
Dangerous amounts of it will remain there

546
00:31:05,941 --> 00:31:09,440
for about 300 years. Engineering Disasters will

547
00:31:09,441 --> 00:31:18,300
return on Modern Marvels. We now return to Engineering

548
00:31:18,301 --> 00:31:26,580
Disasters on Modern Marvels. 25 miles south of Pittsburgh,

549
00:31:26,581 --> 00:31:28,580
in the heart of one of Pennsylvania's industrial

550
00:31:28,581 --> 00:31:33,740
valleys, along the banks of the Monongahela River, 4 million

551
00:31:33,741 --> 00:31:38,840
gallon storage tanks mark the site of a past disaster.

552
00:31:38,841 --> 00:31:42,880
In 1988, when the oil storage facility in Floref, Pennsylvania

553
00:31:42,881 --> 00:31:46,680
was owned by Ashland Oil, different tanks located in the

554
00:31:46,681 --> 00:31:52,840
same spots held fuel oil. On January 2nd of that year, a

555
00:31:52,841 --> 00:31:56,780
typically frigid winter day, a 40-year-old tank was being

556
00:31:56,781 --> 00:31:59,980
refilled for the first time after being disassembled in

557
00:31:59,981 --> 00:32:04,520
in Cleveland, Ohio, and reassembled in Floref.

558
00:32:04,521 --> 00:32:08,560
Then in less than one second, the tank cracked vertically,

559
00:32:08,561 --> 00:32:13,700
releasing a tidal wave of about 3.8 million gallons of oil.

560
00:32:13,701 --> 00:32:17,460
When the tank split, the force of the weight of oil

561
00:32:17,461 --> 00:32:20,480
in the tank pushed the shell of the tank,

562
00:32:20,481 --> 00:32:25,020
the skin of the tank, back against this dike wall

563
00:32:25,021 --> 00:32:25,980
in this direction.

564
00:32:25,981 --> 00:32:33,540
The oil rushed over the containment dikes in front of the split, hurtling toward other

565
00:32:33,541 --> 00:32:34,540
nearby tanks.

566
00:32:34,541 --> 00:32:41,200
When the tank that collapsed, which was behind me, released the oil, the tank that

567
00:32:41,201 --> 00:32:49,760
was formerly in this direction was dented by the force of the oil approximately 20 feet

568
00:32:49,761 --> 00:32:51,100
up on the wall of that tank.

569
00:32:51,101 --> 00:32:55,300
The tank was empty, but it gives you some perspective on how much force there was involved.

570
00:32:55,301 --> 00:32:59,600
While some oil remained inside the dikes, the faster parts of

571
00:32:59,601 --> 00:33:02,600
the flow raced over the final barriers at the south end of the

572
00:33:02,601 --> 00:33:03,480
facility.

573
00:33:03,481 --> 00:33:09,640
When the oil went off to my right, it went over the last

574
00:33:09,641 --> 00:33:12,520
dike in that direction into a parking lot.

575
00:33:12,521 --> 00:33:15,220
The parking lot has a drain in it.

576
00:33:15,221 --> 00:33:20,360
The oil followed that drain into the Monongahela River.

577
00:33:20,361 --> 00:33:24,860
Experts estimated that between 700,000 and 800,000

578
00:33:24,861 --> 00:33:29,160
gallons of oil entered the river, making this the worst oil spill

579
00:33:29,161 --> 00:33:31,460
in the history of this industrialized valley.

580
00:33:34,660 --> 00:33:37,460
The north flowing current of the Monongahela carried the oil

581
00:33:37,461 --> 00:33:40,960
past a number of smaller communities, into Pittsburgh,

582
00:33:40,961 --> 00:33:45,760
and beyond. Towns along the river were forced

583
00:33:45,761 --> 00:33:48,760
to shut off their water intake systems.

584
00:33:48,761 --> 00:33:51,960
Tens of thousands of people were without regular water service

585
00:33:51,961 --> 00:33:54,360
for up to eight days.

586
00:33:54,361 --> 00:33:57,900
Overall, the spill temporarily contaminated drinking water

587
00:33:57,901 --> 00:34:02,200
supplies for about one million people in Pennsylvania, Ohio,

588
00:34:02,201 --> 00:34:05,600
and West Virginia.

589
00:34:05,601 --> 00:34:08,720
Contractors hired by Ashland used booms and other equipment

590
00:34:08,721 --> 00:34:11,320
to collect as much oil as possible from the river's

591
00:34:11,321 --> 00:34:14,120
surface.

592
00:34:14,121 --> 00:34:17,920
By the time John Gross and other investigators arrived,

593
00:34:17,921 --> 00:34:21,960
the oil in the dike areas had been pumped out to barges.

594
00:34:21,961 --> 00:34:25,600
It was very obvious from just standing on one of the dikes,

595
00:34:25,700 --> 00:34:30,700
a full view of the tank, to see that it had split vertically

596
00:34:30,800 --> 00:34:36,640
and had begun to unpeel like you would unpeel an orange.

597
00:34:36,740 --> 00:34:39,540
When the steel split, it left behind arrow-like

598
00:34:39,640 --> 00:34:44,220
markings, similar to the chevron stripes on a military uniform.

599
00:34:44,320 --> 00:34:46,820
We found the chevron markings along this area

600
00:34:46,920 --> 00:34:50,160
to be pointing down, and the chevron markings along this area

601
00:34:50,161 --> 00:34:53,900
to be pointing up. And so just about at eye level

602
00:34:53,901 --> 00:34:57,860
was the confluence of those two, and this is where the fracture

603
00:34:57,861 --> 00:35:01,740
initiated. At that spot was a dark area

604
00:35:01,741 --> 00:35:04,400
about 3 quarters of an inch long.

605
00:35:04,401 --> 00:35:08,240
This is where it had all started. This is a close-up of the

606
00:35:08,241 --> 00:35:11,380
defect in the tank wall. You can see it's this dark area

607
00:35:11,381 --> 00:35:15,820
here, and that dark color is caused by the carburization,

608
00:35:15,821 --> 00:35:20,420
which is a result of the combination of carbon and oxygen,

609
00:35:21,020 --> 00:35:25,500
which is present in the cutting operations that were used in either

610
00:35:25,501 --> 00:35:28,440
putting the tank together the first time or in reassembling.

611
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:34,700
Even today, small defects like this aren't uncommon in these huge tanks.

612
00:35:35,400 --> 00:35:39,280
It's hard to prevent such imperfections during large cutting and welding operations.

613
00:35:39,281 --> 00:35:45,280
But ordinarily, weak spots in modern tanks lead only to small

614
00:35:45,281 --> 00:35:49,780
leaks, which can then be easily repaired and cleaned up.

615
00:35:49,781 --> 00:35:53,280
Because of the speed of the collapse, the clean split, and

616
00:35:53,281 --> 00:35:57,280
the telltale chevron markings, investigators quickly identified

617
00:35:57,281 --> 00:36:00,280
brittle fracture, often a problem with weaker forms of

618
00:36:00,281 --> 00:36:04,280
steel dating from the 1940s and earlier, as the cause of the

619
00:36:04,281 --> 00:36:07,280
catastrophic failure in Fleureff.

620
00:36:07,281 --> 00:36:11,520
Brittle fracture has been known to plague a variety of older

621
00:36:11,521 --> 00:36:17,720
structures, even large ships. Tests revealed it was the main

622
00:36:17,721 --> 00:36:20,720
reason for the failure of this tank, which had been built in

623
00:36:20,721 --> 00:36:24,160
the early 1940s. And the weather compounded the

624
00:36:24,161 --> 00:36:27,320
problem. Subzero temperatures before and

625
00:36:27,321 --> 00:36:30,220
during the filling made the tank's old steel even more

626
00:36:30,221 --> 00:36:33,380
brittle. The exact location of the defect

627
00:36:33,381 --> 00:36:36,980
was also a factor. Welds are always weaker than the

628
00:36:36,981 --> 00:36:41,940
solid inner portions of a plate. When workers disassembled the

629
00:36:41,941 --> 00:36:45,220
tank in Cleveland, new cuts were made away from the original

630
00:36:45,221 --> 00:36:48,580
welds, since re-welding the same area can actually make it

631
00:36:48,581 --> 00:36:52,500
weaker. But when the tank was put back

632
00:36:52,501 --> 00:36:56,020
together in Pennsylvania, the defect happened to be in an

633
00:36:56,021 --> 00:37:00,740
especially dangerous spot. The fracture occurred in this

634
00:37:00,741 --> 00:37:01,926
general location.

635
00:37:01,927 --> 00:37:06,740
This also is the location where a horizontal weld and a vertical weld

636
00:37:06,741 --> 00:37:09,304
come together. We call it a T-joint.

637
00:37:09,305 --> 00:37:13,220
And it's in this area that has particularly high stress

638
00:37:13,221 --> 00:37:18,320
concentrations. Once a tank is built, welds, including

639
00:37:18,321 --> 00:37:19,967
T-joints, are inspected.

640
00:37:19,968 --> 00:37:24,360
To find defects that aren't visible on the surface, like the one

641
00:37:24,361 --> 00:37:30,140
in the Ashland tank, inspectors routinely use an x-ray system for metal, known as radiography.

642
00:37:30,740 --> 00:37:34,380
The joints where the vertical seam and horizontal seam

643
00:37:34,480 --> 00:37:37,320
come together is a typical place where these radiographs

644
00:37:37,420 --> 00:37:38,480
are taken.

645
00:37:38,580 --> 00:37:44,080
That provided the opportunity to possibly identify this defect,

646
00:37:44,180 --> 00:37:46,980
but it was not identified.

647
00:37:47,080 --> 00:37:49,160
Following the investigation of this bill,

648
00:37:49,260 --> 00:37:53,300
state and federal agencies put new procedures in place.

649
00:37:53,400 --> 00:37:56,300
Pennsylvania implemented above-ground storage tank

650
00:37:56,400 --> 00:37:59,700
regulations so that tanks like this tank,

651
00:37:59,701 --> 00:38:04,500
that this tank that failed are inspected routinely now, that

652
00:38:04,501 --> 00:38:08,260
they are tested routinely now in an attempt to avoid situations

653
00:38:08,261 --> 00:38:10,740
like this in the future. The federal government also

654
00:38:10,741 --> 00:38:14,260
changed its oil spill requirements in terms of

655
00:38:14,261 --> 00:38:17,220
equipment that needs to be available and precautions that

656
00:38:17,221 --> 00:38:20,420
need to be taken. A federal grand jury

657
00:38:20,421 --> 00:38:24,100
indicted Ashland Oil for negligently discharging oil into

658
00:38:24,101 --> 00:38:26,980
the Monongahela River. The company eventually paid

659
00:38:26,981 --> 00:38:30,480
almost $7 million in state and federal fines.

660
00:38:35,980 --> 00:38:39,140
Typically, crews are able to recover only about 20%

661
00:38:39,141 --> 00:38:41,720
of the oil spilled on rivers.

662
00:38:41,721 --> 00:38:43,680
That was the approximate percentage recovered

663
00:38:43,681 --> 00:38:48,220
from the 1988 spill in Floraf, Pennsylvania.

664
00:38:48,221 --> 00:38:52,480
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

665
00:38:56,980 --> 00:38:59,580
We now return to Engineering Disasters

666
00:38:59,581 --> 00:39:00,580
on Modern Marvels.

667
00:39:04,020 --> 00:39:08,360
On May 19, 1998, at 6 p.m. Eastern time,

668
00:39:09,300 --> 00:39:11,420
the Galaxy 4 communication satellite

669
00:39:11,421 --> 00:39:13,280
high above the American Midwest

670
00:39:13,281 --> 00:39:16,200
suddenly rotated out of position on its own.

671
00:39:17,540 --> 00:39:20,760
This small movement created a disastrous ripple effect below.

672
00:39:22,620 --> 00:39:26,220
Most of the 40 million pagers in service in the United States

673
00:39:26,221 --> 00:39:31,220
suddenly went silent for the first time in the 35-year history of the industry.

674
00:39:32,380 --> 00:39:36,680
Thousands of ATMs and credit card authorization services also failed.

675
00:39:37,260 --> 00:39:39,780
And several television and radio networks

676
00:39:39,781 --> 00:39:42,680
were unable to distribute programs to affiliates.

677
00:39:43,780 --> 00:39:46,200
There were thousands of antennas on the ground

678
00:39:46,201 --> 00:39:52,520
all expecting to send signals and receive signals back to the Galaxy 4.

679
00:39:52,521 --> 00:39:58,680
If it's pointed the wrong direction, it can't hear what the ground is trying to tell it,

680
00:39:58,740 --> 00:40:01,480
nor can it send signals back to the ground.

681
00:40:02,820 --> 00:40:07,500
More than 80% of the nation's paging companies depended solely on the Galaxy 4.

682
00:40:08,460 --> 00:40:14,680
It took three chaotic days for technicians at Pan Amsat, the operator of the Galaxy 4,

683
00:40:15,000 --> 00:40:19,840
to reroute the millions of pagers to other satellites and restore customer service.

684
00:40:22,520 --> 00:40:25,020
Since the satellite is still in outer space,

685
00:40:25,021 --> 00:40:27,120
it's impossible to inspect.

686
00:40:27,121 --> 00:40:29,720
But the widely accepted cause of this enormous problem

687
00:40:29,721 --> 00:40:34,280
is something incredibly small, a short circuit

688
00:40:34,281 --> 00:40:36,520
within the positioning system created

689
00:40:36,521 --> 00:40:42,020
by a microscopic phenomenon known as a tin whisker.

690
00:40:42,021 --> 00:40:44,520
The small metal leads on microchips

691
00:40:44,521 --> 00:40:46,820
electronically connect the chips to other components

692
00:40:46,821 --> 00:40:50,020
on a circuit board, allowing for the exchange of information

693
00:40:50,021 --> 00:40:54,720
formation across the system. These leads are coated with a

694
00:40:54,721 --> 00:40:58,160
conductive metal to prevent corrosion.

695
00:40:58,161 --> 00:41:01,160
Tin is the preferred coating material because it's widely

696
00:41:01,161 --> 00:41:06,680
available and inexpensive. Tin whiskers are crystalline

697
00:41:06,681 --> 00:41:10,180
structures that grow spontaneously from this coating

698
00:41:10,181 --> 00:41:12,880
and can short-circuit microchip components by suddenly

699
00:41:12,881 --> 00:41:17,720
connecting leads that aren't supposed to be connected.

700
00:41:17,721 --> 00:41:21,520
Usually these leads are only 75 microns apart,

701
00:41:21,521 --> 00:41:24,100
meaning electron microscopes are needed to get a good look

702
00:41:24,101 --> 00:41:25,660
at the whiskers.

703
00:41:25,661 --> 00:41:28,900
This is a picture of two whiskers and maybe a third whisker

704
00:41:28,901 --> 00:41:30,800
on the side here.

705
00:41:30,801 --> 00:41:33,000
To give you a sense of the dimensions,

706
00:41:33,001 --> 00:41:36,440
these two whiskers are about, oh, maybe a quarter

707
00:41:36,441 --> 00:41:40,780
or at least less than a half the diameter of a human hair.

708
00:41:40,781 --> 00:41:44,320
And the lengths are about 100 microns.

709
00:41:44,321 --> 00:41:50,520
And this length dimension would cross between two leads of this

710
00:41:50,521 --> 00:41:54,520
kind of a component here.

711
00:41:54,521 --> 00:41:58,160
The tin whisker that caused the Galaxy 4 to fail grew from the

712
00:41:58,161 --> 00:42:00,880
best intentions.

713
00:42:00,881 --> 00:42:03,720
Researchers identified whisker growth on tin and other metals

714
00:42:03,721 --> 00:42:07,260
as early as the 1940s.

715
00:42:07,261 --> 00:42:10,880
During the 1950s, experiments revealed that adding lead to

716
00:42:10,881 --> 00:42:15,380
created an alloy that greatly reduced whisker growth.

717
00:42:15,381 --> 00:42:18,680
But environmental concerns about lead caused many electronics

718
00:42:18,681 --> 00:42:24,120
manufacturers to switch back to pure tin, beginning in the 1980s.

719
00:42:24,121 --> 00:42:26,800
Tin whiskers have been blamed for the complete loss of three

720
00:42:26,801 --> 00:42:31,760
satellites, and have partially damaged at least four others.

721
00:42:31,761 --> 00:42:35,040
But satellites aren't the only type of expensive and critical

722
00:42:35,041 --> 00:42:37,680
equipment that these tiny gremlins strike.

723
00:42:37,681 --> 00:42:43,520
They've shown up in F-15 fighter aircraft radar,

724
00:42:43,620 --> 00:42:48,420
shown up in air-to-air missiles, heart pacemakers.

725
00:42:48,520 --> 00:42:51,420
In the nuclear power industry, the tin whisker problem

726
00:42:51,520 --> 00:42:55,460
showed up in the unplanned shutdown of the Millstone 3

727
00:42:55,560 --> 00:42:58,560
plant in April 2005 in Connecticut.

728
00:42:58,660 --> 00:43:02,080
That problem was due to a tin whisker crossing over

729
00:43:02,180 --> 00:43:06,440
between circuits in a logic board.

730
00:43:06,441 --> 00:43:09,040
Inside the University of Maryland's engineering

731
00:43:09,041 --> 00:43:11,840
department, long-term experiments are conducted

732
00:43:11,841 --> 00:43:15,880
over several years by subjecting closely spaced plates of tin

733
00:43:15,881 --> 00:43:18,880
and other metals to various temperature, humidity,

734
00:43:18,881 --> 00:43:21,480
and other conditions.

735
00:43:21,481 --> 00:43:23,400
From these experiments, researchers

736
00:43:23,401 --> 00:43:26,400
can determine if certain conditions inhibit or promote

737
00:43:26,401 --> 00:43:27,101
whisker growth.

738
00:43:29,800 --> 00:43:33,000
These plates are being subjected to 50% humidity

739
00:43:33,001 --> 00:43:36,400
at 50 degrees Celsius.

740
00:43:36,401 --> 00:43:38,800
and pulling one out, if you look,

741
00:43:38,801 --> 00:43:43,500
basically we have a span of about 75 microns

742
00:43:43,501 --> 00:43:46,240
spacing between these two plates.

743
00:43:46,241 --> 00:43:51,960
If we take it over here, using this resistance measurement,

744
00:43:51,961 --> 00:43:55,400
if we haven't opened, basically we'll record nothing.

745
00:43:55,401 --> 00:44:00,000
If we touch these two surfaces, if there is a whisker,

746
00:44:00,001 --> 00:44:02,100
we'll see a response, and as you can see,

747
00:44:02,101 --> 00:44:04,860
a response is indicated, which means there is a whisker

748
00:44:04,861 --> 00:44:08,600
bridging these two surfaces.

749
00:44:08,601 --> 00:44:10,660
But scientists still don't understand

750
00:44:10,661 --> 00:44:12,500
how to prevent these formations from occurring

751
00:44:12,501 --> 00:44:15,600
in the first place.

752
00:44:15,601 --> 00:44:18,080
Meanwhile, as electronics and microchips continue

753
00:44:18,081 --> 00:44:22,540
to get smaller and appear in more and more everyday products,

754
00:44:22,541 --> 00:44:25,340
the tin whiskers problem keeps getting bigger.

755
00:44:25,341 --> 00:44:29,380
Tin whiskers are a classic example of a very small thing

756
00:44:29,381 --> 00:44:32,080
causing a great deal of damage.

757
00:44:32,081 --> 00:44:34,620
They have caused hundreds of millions of dollars

758
00:44:34,621 --> 00:44:39,420
and are capable of causing damage in the billions.

